****Two Pointer Technique****
      When to use it

      Use two pointers when:
      The data is in an array/string.
      You need to compare elements from both ends or maintain a window.
      A brute-force solution is O(n²).

      Common problems:
      Container With Most Water
      Two Sum (sorted array)
      Trapping Rain Water
      Remove Duplicates
      Palindrome checking
      Merge two sorted arrays

      Two Pointers: Use two indices to scan an array efficiently instead of checking every pair.
      When to use: Sorted arrays, opposite-end comparisons, or when brute force is O(n²).
      Container With Most Water: Start at both ends, compute the area, then move the pointer with the smaller height because the width always decreases and only a taller minimum height can increase the area.
      Sorted Two Sum: Compare the sum; if it's too small, move the left pointer; if it's too large, move the right pointer.
      Sliding Window (a two-pointer variant): Expand the right pointer to include elements and move the left pointer to maintain the required condition.
      Time Complexity: Usually O(n) because each pointer moves at most n times.
      Space Complexity: Usually O(1).
